OSTree is a system for managing bootable, immutable, versioned
filesystem trees. It is used both as the foundation for operating
system build and deployment systems (such as Fedora Silverblue,
Endless OS, and GNOME Continuous) and as a standalone tool for
managing container/OS image repositories.

It supports:
- Storing files indexed by their content hash (CAS), with hardlink
  deduplication.
- Signed commits and static delta transfers.
- GPG and ed25519 signature verification.
- A libostree shared library (libostree-1) and the `ostree` CLI.

FreeBSD notes:
This port builds libostree-1 and the `ostree` command-line tool on
FreeBSD. The repository core (init, commit, pull, pull-local, checkout,
fsck, show, log, summary, static deltas and GPG verification) works and
is exercised by the port's smoke test.

The Linux extended-attribute API is provided by a FreeBSD extattr(2)
shim, and the ext2 immutable-flag ioctls are reimplemented with
fchflags(2). Features that depend on Linux kernel primitives - mount
namespaces, pivot_root, overlayfs, kexec, fs-verity, O_TMPFILE,
rofiles-fuse and systemd - are not supported: the affected code is
guarded so that it builds and reports "not supported" at runtime. In
particular, `ostree admin` deployment/sysroot and bootloader management
remain Linux-only and do not work on FreeBSD.
